<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Azaz</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as 'Azaz</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Azaz is a town in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> near the Turkish border. In 1030 the Mirdasid emir of Aleppo defeated the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanos-iii-argyros">Romanos III Argyros</a> near it, and its castle was later fought over by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> of the County of Edessa and Muslim rulers; the Crusaders won a battle there in 1125. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syrian-civil-war">Syrian civil war</a> it became a stronghold of the opposition under Turkish protection.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/azaz.jpg" alt="Azaz" width="320" height="500" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Brian Dell · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mosque_in_A%27zaz.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Azaz lies in north-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, roughly 32 kilometres north-north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, near the border crossing into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> at Öncüpınar, south of Kilis. In ancient times it was known in Hurrian as Azazuwa, in Old Aramaic as Ḥzz, in Neo-Assyrian as Ḫazazu and in Medieval Greek as Azázion. Crusader sources call it Hazart.</p><p>Its topography and its position overlooking the surrounding region gave it strategic importance in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> era. In Muslim hands it blocked communications between the Crusader states of Edessa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>, and in Crusader hands it threatened the major Muslim city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Excavations at Tell Azaz have yielded large quantities of ceramics from the early and middle Islamic periods, though the settlement is rarely mentioned in Islamic texts before the 12th century. The musician Ishaq al-Mawsili (767 to 850) visited it under Abbasid rule, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamdanid-dynasty">Hamdanids</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> built a brick citadel on the tell. On 10 August 1030 the Mirdasids defeated the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanos-iii-argyros">Romanos III</a> at nearby Tubbal. In December of that year the Byzantine generals Niketas of Mistheia and Symeon besieged and captured Azaz and burned Tubbal.</p><p>Around December 1118 Roger of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> and the Armenian prince Leo I took Azaz from the Turcoman prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilghazi">Ilghazi</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardin">Mardin</a>. In April 1125 Aqsunqur al-Bursuqi of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> and Toghtekin invaded the Principality of Antioch and surrounded the town. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> coalition of 3,000 under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-ii-of-jerusalem">Baldwin II of Jerusalem</a> confronted a Muslim coalition of 15,000 and defeated it at the Battle of Azaz, raising the siege. After the Zengid capture of Edessa in 1144 the fortresses of the County of Edessa were gradually neglected. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humphrey-ii-of-toron">Humphrey II of Toron</a> sent sixty knights to reinforce Azaz in 1146, but the fortress fell to Nur ad-Din, the Zengid emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, in June 1150.</p><p>The Ayyubid emir al-Aziz Uthman rebuilt the Hamdanid structure in stone. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaqut-al-hamawi">Yaqut al-Hamawi</a> called it a fine town in 1226, and it was the centre of a district that included Kafr Latha, Mannagh, Yabrin, Arfad, Tubbal and Innib. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a> ruled from the 13th century and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> entered the area in 1516, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-marj-dabiq">Battle of Marj Dabiq</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> fell, the new border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> ran just north of Azaz. The town was part of the French Mandate for Syria and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> and, from 1946, of independent Syria. The 2004 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> gave it a population of 31,623. Rebels captured the town in July 2012 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syrian-civil-war">Syrian civil war</a>, and from 2016 it lay in an area under Turkish control.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Azaz” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Near the Syrian town of <b>Azaz</b>, the forces of the Mirdasid emir of Aleppo, Shibl al-Dawla Nasr, defeated the much larger Byzantine army of Emperor Romanos III Argyros.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-10">10 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Salih was killed in 1029 at al-Uqhuwana, but his son Shibl al-Dawla Nasr defeated the Byzantine emperor Romanos III near <b>Azaz</b> in 1030 and then accepted Byzantine protection.</blockquote><a href="/state/mirdasid-emirate">Mirdasid Emirate</a></li></ul></section>
